WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.800
- [Narrator] There's been a domino effect

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.565
in the collapse of the crypto industry.

00:00:04.565 --> 00:00:05.398
(dominoes falling)

00:00:05.398 --> 00:00:07.890
The fall of the stable
coin Terra sparked a wave

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.650
of losses leading to bankruptcy filings

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.040
for five major crypto
companies, paused withdrawals

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.520
and declining value for
cryptocurrencies but-

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.870
- The contagion effects

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.970
to the wider economy have been limited

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.880
so there's like massive carnage over here

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.920
but not much impact over here.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.841
- [Narrator] Usually when
an industry collapses,

00:00:27.841 --> 00:00:29.040
(dominoes falling)

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.800
it has the potential to
ripple far out to other parts

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.460
of the economy and can even
spark a financial crisis.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.470
But so far, for crypto the fallout

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.540
has been pretty contained.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.460
Here's why the crypto industries collapse

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.459
has happened in a closed loop.

00:00:43.459 --> 00:00:45.720
(dominoes falling)

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:49.530
The finances of many crypto
companies are intertwined.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.660
- It's a very incestuous kind of scene

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.390
where you have the same
companies lending to each other,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.330
borrowing from each other, and
then trading with each other.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.370
- [Narrator] Just looking
at a slice of the industry,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.960
you can see how closely related

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.450
those five bankrupt companies were.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:06.990
Three Arrows, a crypto focused
hedge fund, had invested

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.820
in crypto assets and companies

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.250
and took loans out from other companies.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.700
One of these investments was
Terra's sister token Luna.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.140
When these assets fell,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.630
so did Three Arrows' balance sheet.

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:21.450
As a result, the company
failed to repay debts

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.880
and was ordered to liquidate in June.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:25.650
In the wake of that bankruptcy,

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:27.900
crypto lenders, BlockFi, Voyager

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.910
and Celsius were taking losses

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.190
on declining digital currency prices

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.800
and on loans they had
given to Three Arrows.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.350
Then Crypto Exchange FTX extended a line

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.630
of credit to BlockFi
and its sister company

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.030
Alameda extended a line to Voyager.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.700
Voyager filed for bankruptcy in July.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.670
A few days later, Celsius did too

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:50.130
and when FTX filed for
bankruptcy in November

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.240
due to a mismanagement of
funds, BlockFi followed.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:55.950
- It's like these
companies are all wearing

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.660
these different hats with each other

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.410
so you can see how these
companies are so interconnected

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.960
and so intermingled that it sets the stage

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.600
for contagion to have a
really devastating effect.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.820
- [Narrator] The Financial
Stability Oversight Council

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.620
said in a report in October

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.480
that this interconnectedness is part

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.673
of why the industry
can crumble so quickly.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.334
One of the reasons those
companies are so tightly linked,

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:20.880
traditional finance
institutions have stayed

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.010
pretty separated from crypto.

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:24.600
For example, if you look at the list

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.640
of some of Celsius' biggest creditors

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.120
in their bankruptcy filing,
there aren't any banks listed.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.250
- In other industries, for example,

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:36.300
banks will provide credit lines
or arrange corporate bonds

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.980
or do other sorts of financings.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:40.050
In crypto it's been much more limited.

00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:40.883
There just hasn't been

00:02:40.883 --> 00:02:44.070
the same embrace that
other industries have seen.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:45.660
- [Narrator] A small handful of banks

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.570
like these have courted crypto companies,

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.490
but big players in the financial market

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.720
have been more hesitant.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:52.860
- Okay, I'm a major skeptic

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:57.150
on crypto tokens, which you
call currency like Bitcoin.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.250
- [Narrator] Regulation for
banks which is helped set

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.680
by the Office of the
Comptroller of the Currency

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.900
requires them to have a
number of safety measures

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.690
in place before engaging
with digital assets.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.790
- The resilience of the
traditional banking system

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.030
to the recent events in
crypto is not an accident.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.865
Rather, it is due, at least in part

00:03:14.865 --> 00:03:17.250
to federal bank regulators continued

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.470
and intentional emphasis on safety

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.660
and soundness and consumer protection.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.850
- [Narrator] Crypto makes up just 0.01%

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:25.620
of exposure for banks.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:28.200
- Since the traditional
Wall Street banks have not

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.530
really participated in
the space, it's been left

00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:34.470
to the crypto companies themselves to act

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:37.500
as like defacto banks within the space.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:40.770
You see how these companies
have become so interconnected

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.230
and intermingled that the failure of one

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:44.910
can bring down the rest.

00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.890
- [Narrator] But that
isolation has helped prevent

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:50.160
the crypto crisis from spilling
into the larger economy.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.500
Think about the housing crash in 2007

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.660
that eventually led to
the Great Recession.

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.190
When housing prices declined,

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.890
it sparked a crisis in subprime loans.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.980
As some banks had a stake
in these type of mortgages,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.893
that crisis leaked into the
traditional financial system.

00:04:05.804 --> 00:04:08.910
- It began to snowball and
cause a system-wide meltdown

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.910
and major financial institutions
like Lehman Brothers

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:14.880
failed because of their
exposure to the housing market.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.190
- [Narrator] Other industries like housing

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.450
also have value in the economy.

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.340
There's dollars behind it,

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.520
but crypto's value is
still mostly speculative.

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.560
- This is what happens when you have a lot

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:29.220
of money pouring into
an unregulated industry

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:32.850
that has little discernible clear value

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.980
aside from the speculation itself.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.900
- [Narrator] While the crash
has been mostly contained

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.090
in this case, it's left a lot of questions

00:04:39.090 --> 00:04:41.790
about how financial
institutions might interact

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:43.390
with the industry in the future.

